<GrowthPage title="Synthesizer + Visualizer" description="A MIDI-controlled Pure Data instrument connecting sound and visuals through shared controls.">
<header class="case-intro"><a class="quiet-link" href="/projects/">← Back to Work</a><p class="eyebrow">Creative instrument / Class project / 2024</p><h1>One gesture.<br /><em>Two ways to understand it.</em></h1><p class="work-lead">A synthesizer, arpeggiator, and visualizer controlled through a MIDI keyboard. Built in Pure Data with GEM.</p><div class="case-facts"><div><span>My role</span>Design &amp; implementation</div><div><span>Built with</span>Pure Data · GEM · MIDI</div><div><span>Project status</span>Completed class exploration</div></div><a class="button" href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the original project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The starting point</p><h2>Follow the idea.<br />Find a workable scope.</h2><p>I began with an idea for a vocoder that would translate an audio sample to nearby notes in a C major scale. I also wanted to explore synthesis and visuals with GEM.</p><p>The vocoder remained partial. The implemented class project became a synthesizer with effects, an arpeggiator, and a visualizer.</p><p class="source-note">From my original project commentary, dated April 27, 2024. <a href={source+'/PD%20Synth%20Readme.pdf'} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the project notes ↗</a></p></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The interaction</p><h2>A physical control.<br />More than one response.</h2><p>Play a note on the MIDI keyboard, then turn a knob. The control changes the audio and a corresponding property of the visual scene.</p><div class="mapping-table"><table><caption>Examples documented in the original instrument</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Control</th><th scope="col">What you hear</th><th scope="col">What you see</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mappings.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,i)=>i===0?<th scope="row">{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>The eight audio controls cover volume, tempo, glide, resonance/cutoff, reverb, chorus, delay, and panning. Visual mappings differ between the shape and particle scenes.</p><div class="case-callout"><h3>The arpeggiator</h3><p>With the arpeggiator enabled, play four notes and hold the last one to keep the sequence repeating. For example: B, C, F, G. Hold G to continue the pattern; release it to let the notes decay.</p></div></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Building the system</p><h2>Separate the parts.<br />Keep the signals connected.</h2><p>I organized the patch into reusable abstractions for synthesis, effects, the visualizer, and GEM objects. In my original notes, I described how grouping related components made the patch easier to extend.</p><div class="implementation-grid"><div><h3>MIDI input</h3><p>Keyboard input and controller values enter the patch. The source includes note input and a four-pitch array for the arpeggiator.</p><code>notein · stripnote · $0-pitches</code></div><div><h3>Audio processing</h3><p>Subpatches organize effects such as reverb and delay around the synthesizer.</p><code>rev2~ · delwrite~ · delread~</code></div><div><h3>Visual response</h3><p>GEM scenes translate controls into changes in size, position, motion, and particle behavior.</p><code>gemhead · scaleXYZ · translateXYZ</code></div></div><p class="source-note">Object names above come from the original patch. <a href={source+'/VirtKeyboard.pd'} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect the source ↗</a></p></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">04 / What the work shows</p><h2>A working exploration.<br />Room to keep experimenting.</h2><p>The project produced a Pure Data patch and a written guide for playing it through a MIDI controller. It demonstrates how I connect interaction, sound, visuals, and modular implementation.</p><p>It was a creative class project, not a validated learning or therapeutic tool. Some visual mappings remain unfinished. The original notes identify further effects, richer scenes, and a possible audio plugin as future directions.</p><details class="work-details"><summary>Run the original project</summary><ol><li>Install Pure Data and the GEM library.</li><li>Open <code>VirtKeyboard.pd</code> from the repository.</li><li>Connect your MIDI controller and configure MIDI input in Pure Data.</li><li>Match the controller’s knobs to the patch’s mappings, then try notes and effects.</li></ol><p>The patch was built around an eight-knob controller. Your controller may need different assignments. This case study does not connect to MIDI devices or run the original patch in your browser.</p><a class="quiet-link" href={source+'/README.md'} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the original guide ↗</a></details></section>
<section class="case-section case-next"><p class="eyebrow">The connection</p><h2>Make an idea easier to explore.</h2><p>Sound and visuals offer different ways into the same interaction. That interest continues in the tools I’m building for attention and for understanding data.</p><a class="button" href="/projects/#daily-focus-coach">See what I’m building now <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
